excited to be here.

00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:35.800
BRIAN DORSEY: So conversational
AI-- what is that?

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:37.330
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Conversational AI

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:40.150
or conversational experiences--

00:05:40.150 --> 00:05:42.400
it's basically a
comprehensive name

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:47.690
for a newer class of solutions
that include chat bots, voice

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:48.760
bots--

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:51.610
experiences that are
basically conversational

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:54.400
based, so whether it's
in apps or messengers

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:55.720
and things like that.

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:58.120
The common denominator
is basically

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:00.460
the use of natural
language understanding

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:03.580
and natural language
processing, which is obviously

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:06.640
a type of AI technology,
to power these interactions

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:10.075
between humans and machines.

00:06:10.075 --> 00:06:12.440
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So let's
take it a step back there.

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:15.320
Tell us what natural language
understanding really means.

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:16.570
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Absolutely.

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:20.530
So in very simplistic terms,
natural language understanding

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:25.150
is a technology that helps
translate human language

00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:28.240
into computer language--
and also vice versa.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:30.620
So let's think about
this as an example.

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:33.160
So when you and I-- when
two humans are talking

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:36.080
to each other, if we speak
in the same language,

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:37.310
we don't need a translator.

00:06:37.310 --> 00:06:40.380
But if I speak English and
you speak, say, Spanish,

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:43.000
and I don't understand
Spanish and vice versa,

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:44.800
then we need a
translator in the middle

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:47.020
to translate my
English to Spanish

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:49.060
and then your
Spanish to English.

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:54.130
Same way when humans are talking
that human language needs

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:57.970
to be translated to a computer,
because a computer speaks

00:06:57.970 --> 00:06:58.480
binary--

00:06:58.480 --> 00:06:59.500
0's and 1's.

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:01.900
So our English needs
to translate into those

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:02.650
0's and 1's.

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:04.870
And then the computer
responds back.

00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:07.030
Then that binary needs
to be translated back

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:08.720
into English as well.

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:12.910
So that technology can be called
natural language understanding.

00:07:12.910 --> 00:07:15.880
And then while we are at it,
natural language processing

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:18.490
is also worth mentioning.

00:07:18.490 --> 00:07:20.830
Because natural
language processing

00:07:20.830 --> 00:07:24.340
is a superset of this realm,
where natural language

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:26.170
understanding falls in it.

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:28.060
And natural language
processing includes

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:30.790
things like, how do you
paraphrase something?

00:07:30.790 --> 00:07:33.970
Or how do you summarize
a given story?

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:36.130
Or how do you
understand sentiments

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:37.630
off of a conversation?

00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:42.130
Or was there a spelling
mistake or an error or accent

00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:44.570
changes and differences
and things like that?

00:07:44.570 --> 00:07:47.420
So that is what natural language
understanding and processing

00:07:47.420 --> 00:07:47.920
means.

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:51.012
And it's kind of the core of
conversational AI technology.

00:07:51.012 --> 00:07:51.720
BRIAN DORSEY: OK.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:54.570
So this is more than just
speech to text, then?

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:55.660
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yes.

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:58.330
It's absolutely much
more than speech to text.

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:01.120
Like I said, when you talk about
natural language processing,

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:03.803
speech to text is
one part of it.

00:08:03.803 --> 00:08:05.470
There is natural
language understanding,

00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:06.910
which is the other part.

00:08:06.910 --> 00:08:10.060
And then you hear things
like text to speech

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:11.500
is another part of it.

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:14.570
And then you'll also hear
automatic speech recognition,

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:15.310
which is ASR.

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:17.710
So these are kind
of different parts

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:20.110
of the technology that
fall under natural language

00:08:20.110 --> 00:08:20.610
processing.

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:23.170
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So
we've got a way for people

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:26.293
to talk and understand
what they're saying.

00:08:26.293 --> 00:08:27.710
And that's kind
of one side of it.

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:30.280
But then you dive deeper
into it with things

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:32.679
like sentiment and understanding
what they're saying--

00:08:32.679 --> 00:08:35.230
even going so far as to
summarize what they're saying,

00:08:35.230 --> 00:08:38.890
because you're able to pull out
these very abstract concepts

00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:42.400
and then define them and say, I
think when this person mentions

00:08:42.400 --> 00:08:44.890
something about the Eiffel
Tower, for instance,

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:46.028
I know what that is.

00:08:46.028 --> 00:08:46.820
I know where it is.

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:47.903
I know it's this landmark.

00:08:47.903 --> 00:08:50.830
And I can use that
information to pull together

00:08:50.830 --> 00:08:53.250
something else-- whatever
that workflow might be.

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:55.000
Going back to what you
were saying earlier

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:58.240
about chat bots, on
a website, usually

00:08:58.240 --> 00:08:59.830
there's a pop-up or
something and then

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:03.520
you can kind of converse
with, in this case, an AI.

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:06.160
So this is something
new or interesting.

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:09.180
We've been talking about
robo chat for a while,

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:11.680
but this is more
about actual AI.

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:12.580
Why is this big?

00:09:12.580 --> 00:09:15.238
And what's happening now that's
making this more prevalent

00:09:15.238 --> 00:09:16.030
than it used to be?

00:09:16.030 --> 00:09:17.655
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
The world that we're

00:09:17.655 --> 00:09:20.650
living in now with more
and more devices that

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:22.670
are coming online these days--

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:26.380
whether it's IoT or
just our preference

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:30.510
of dealing with more devices
and working with more devices--

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:31.660
take an example.

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:35.210
You wake up in the morning
starting from a smart coffee

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:38.440
maker to when you end
your day with asking

00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:40.060
Google to set your alarm.

00:09:40.060 --> 00:09:42.860
You deal with so many
devices in the day.

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:45.250
And it's going to
be, and it already

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:49.630
is, becoming really difficult
to deal with all the devices

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:52.530
and work with them
on a smart screen.

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:55.990
We are going to have to have
conversations with our machines

00:09:55.990 --> 00:09:59.020
and devices as we
progress in this era

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:00.910
where we have more
and more devices.

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:03.850
Just an example-- when you're
having breakfast or cooking

00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:06.250
breakfast and you
want to know what's

00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:09.610
the traffic to the office, you
don't want to look at a screen.

00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:12.020
You just want to know
the answer to that.

00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:13.850
So that's just one
example, but that

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:16.500
goes to say that we
are moving in the era

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:20.700
where dealing with machines is
becoming our everyday pattern

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:22.240
and every minute pattern.

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:24.870
And for those
reasons, most people

00:10:24.870 --> 00:10:29.400
are interested in having their
problems solved with voice

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:31.075
and conversational interfaces.

00:10:31.075 --> 00:10:33.450
BRIAN DORSEY: So this is
showing up in a bunch of places.

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:34.867
You mentioned chat
bots, and we've

00:10:34.867 --> 00:10:37.380
got these kind of boxes you
