or other assistant thing

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:41.970
in the morning for weather
and traffic and such.

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:43.470
Are these the most
common use cases,

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:44.980
or are there other things?

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:47.250
What are people actually
doing with this today,

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:48.990
and what do you see
them doing next?

00:10:48.990 --> 00:10:49.990
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah.

00:10:49.990 --> 00:10:53.100
So I just gave the broad
examples with the traffic

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:55.110
pattern and alarms and such--

00:10:55.110 --> 00:10:56.110
and smart coffee makers.

00:10:56.110 --> 00:10:57.960
But what companies
or enterprises

00:10:57.960 --> 00:11:01.200
are using conversational
AI for are

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:03.510
use cases around customer
experience and customer

00:11:03.510 --> 00:11:04.560
support.

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:07.510
And it's very easy to
start with that use case.

00:11:07.510 --> 00:11:09.660
It's because of
the fact that you

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:13.290
know a very specific
pattern of questions

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:16.530
that your customers would
ask or your users would ask.

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:20.400
So it's the lowest barrier to
entry into conversational AI.

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:22.230
And examples could include--

00:11:22.230 --> 00:11:24.030
for example, if you
talk about retail,

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:27.000
that customer experience could
be a personal shopper, where

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:31.200
I want to know a specific type
of outerwear I'm looking for.

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:33.030
And then this personal
shopper can give me

00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:36.210
recommendations on here are
some of the different sizes

00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:39.930
and colors and party wearers
versus others and things

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:40.500
like that.

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:43.810
And I actually built a demo
around that a few weeks ago.

00:11:43.810 --> 00:11:45.750
And then the other
example could be

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:48.330
in banking or in
financial institutions

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:50.340
where, really, when
you go to a teller,

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:52.620
you ask questions like,
what's my balance,

00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:54.690
or I want to withdraw
an amount, or I

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:57.630
want to transfer an amount from
this account to this account.

00:11:57.630 --> 00:11:59.940
And you can do all
of that through chat

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:01.540
or a conversational experience.

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:03.900
So those are some
of the easy ways

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:06.630
to kind of get into it
and also the best place

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:09.960
to start, because you know
what the user is asking for

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:13.680
and you know how to respond to
it because your back-ends are

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:16.380
already supporting
that with your websites

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:17.880
or in a more
personalized manner.

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:19.830
So you can put
those two together

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:23.580
into a conversational experience
by using a natural language

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:25.220
understanding or
processing platform

00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:27.720
like the one we're going to
talk about, which is Dialogflow.

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:28.440
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Don't worry-- we'll

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:30.120
mention Dialogflow for you.

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:32.730
But I wanted to pick up
on some of the topics you

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:33.720
were saying there.

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:35.460
With customer support
being probably one

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:38.190
of the premier examples
of this and basically

00:12:38.190 --> 00:12:41.100
just taking that conversation
piece and automating it--

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:44.340
because there's a huge subset
of the users that call in

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:45.412
or they contact support.

00:12:45.412 --> 00:12:47.370
And I'm sure there's some
percentage out there.

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:51.930
I'll make one up and say 98%
can be solved through routing it

00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:55.440
through three simple kind of
formula questions that are FAQs

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:56.470
or what have you.

00:12:56.470 --> 00:12:58.630
But then the people who do
kind of get through that

00:12:58.630 --> 00:13:01.265
and when they do get to
usually a live human agent,

00:13:01.265 --> 00:13:03.390
they'll at least have a
little bit more information

00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:04.938
on what the context is.

00:13:04.938 --> 00:13:06.480
So there's this
great kind of balance

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:09.080
between not having to be on
hold as long because you don't

00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:10.180
have to wait for a person.

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:12.150
Many people can interface with
a machine at the same time

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:13.480
and not have to overload it.

00:13:13.480 --> 00:13:15.660
But we've seen implementations
of these things

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:17.100
for a long time, right?

00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:18.720
The automated phone
support system

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:20.550
has been around
for quite a while.

00:13:20.550 --> 00:13:24.240
And I speak for myself
and maybe popular media

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:28.170
when we say like, oh, these
are so frustrating sometimes.

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:29.370
And they're so rigid.

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:33.228
And you're just like, I need
this piece of information,

00:13:33.228 --> 00:13:34.770
but the machine
won't let me have it.

00:13:34.770 --> 00:13:37.080
Or it needs these 80 things
that are not related.

00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:37.930
It's very cool.

00:13:37.930 --> 00:13:38.860
It's very interesting.

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:40.810
But it can also be
incredibly frustrating.

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:43.980
I would hope that a lot of
this stuff is changing it.

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:46.440
But I'm sure there's been
lots of failure cases as well.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:48.112
Where does the
challenge lie in this?

00:13:48.112 --> 00:13:49.320
Where do the problems happen?

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:51.612
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yeah, I
think the biggest thing that

00:13:51.612 --> 00:13:53.670
needs to be thought
about are why

00:13:53.670 --> 00:13:58.050
conversational interfaces have
failed or do actually fail

00:13:58.050 --> 00:13:59.220
even today.

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:04.710
There is a really good
compiled list, chatbots.fail,

00:14:04.710 --> 00:14:07.770
and it's definitely
worth checking out.

00:14:07.770 --> 00:14:10.350
But I'm going to
highlight a few here.

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:13.590
Some of the reasons why
chat bots actually fail

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:17.070
is the rigid structure--
the flow charts.

00:14:17.070 --> 00:14:20.730
They're really designed for
how the machine responds

00:14:20.730 --> 00:14:22.410
and what the
machine's looking for,

00:14:22.410 --> 00:14:25.500
not how the human
would say something.

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:27.960
And that is where the
biggest problem is.

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:30.870
So what we need to
do in order to create

00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:33.262
a good natural
experience is to use

00:14:33.262 --> 00:14:35.220
natural language,
obviously-- natural language,

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:36.780
understanding, and
processing-- where

00:14:36.780 --> 00:14:39.120
you can have the
machine understand

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:43.170
what the user is saying and then
decide what needs to be done.

00:14:43.170 --> 00:14:45.270
Versus in the IVR
examples that are

00:14:45.270 --> 00:14:47.490
frustrating that you
mentioned, Mark, we

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:48.930
went the other way around.

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:52.660
We are basically designing
an IVR by saying, OK,

00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:57.070
I will only take a customer
account information.

00:14:57.070 --> 00:14:59.430
And if they don't give me
the account information,

00:14:59.430 --> 00:15:02.070
I'm going to keep saying,
can you say that again?

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:03.570
Can you say that again?

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:05.490
And that is what
makes it frustrating,

00:15:05.490 --> 00:15:08.760
because there is no
understanding that the customer

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:10.320
doesn't have that.

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:14.020
So I can ask for
these other things.

00:15:14.020 --> 00:15:17.100
I'll give you a very simple
example where if I say,

00:15:17.100 --> 00:15:21.420
would you like coffee, and the
IVR is expecting yes or no,

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:24.330
and if I said, sure, it's gone.

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:26.700
It's gone wild,
because it doesn't know

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:29.420
that "sure" also means a yes.

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:30.990
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
I got to be honest.

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:33.990
In the mornings when I
need coffee, even yes or no

00:15:33.990 --> 00:15:35.970
is asking a lot.

00:15:35.970 --> 00:15:39.450
If I could just kind of
unintelligibly grunt towards it

00:15:39.450 --> 00:15:41.873
and it's like, yeah,
he needs coffee,

00:15:41.873 --> 00:15:42.915
that would be preferable.

00:15:42.915 --> 00:15:44.040
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Exactly.

00:15:44.040 --> 00:15:45.690
So that's what I'm alluding to.

00:15:45.690 --> 00:15:48.600
And that's the
advancement in technology

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:52.050
that we are utilizing with
natural language understanding

00:15:52.050 --> 00:15:52.688
and processing.

00:15:52.688 --> 00:15:53.730
So that's definitely one.

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:56.100
And then some of
the others are you

00:15:56.100 --> 00:15:59.490
need to make sure that your
back-end is actually properly

00:15:59.490 --> 00:16:01.770
integrated with your front-end.

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:04.650
So you can have the very best
natural language understanding

00:16:04.650 --> 00:16:08.100
experience, but if your
back-ends or your databases

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:11.700
cannot connect properly to
respond to these questions,

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:14.410
then again that experience
is not going to be useful.

00:16:14.410 --> 00:16:16.230
So in this case,
let's take an example

00:16:16.230 --> 00:16:18.660
and say you connect
to Salesforce back-end

00:16:18.660 --> 00:16:21.600
where you create these
tickets or questions

00:16:21.600 --> 00:16:22.770
that the user comes with.

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:24.570
And you create a
case in Salesforce.

00:16:24.570 --> 00:16:28.530
But the hook of the API that
needs to make that connection

00:16:28.530 --> 00:16:32.820
is either not live or you
don't have the right way

00:16:32.820 --> 00:16:34.680
of making that connection.

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:38.050
Then it's not going to be a
really good and streamlined

00:16:38.050 --> 00:16:38.550
experience.

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:40.950
So making sure that the
back-end can connect

00:16:40.950 --> 00:16:43.110
is another very big example.

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:45.390
And then some others
could be creating

00:16:45.390 --> 00:16:47.490
a chat bot that is a silo.

00:16:47.490 --> 00:16:51.690
And it only does this one thing
and doesn't do the other five

00:16:51.690 --> 00:16:53.980
things that it should be doing.

00:16:53.980 --> 00:16:57.510
So if somebody asks a question
about the other five things

00:16:57.510 --> 00:17:01.200
that are not handled, then how
do you handle them in that bot?

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:03.720
It's OK to not
have them handled--

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:06.280
not have the answers
to those questions--

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:09.790
but it's not OK to
leave the user thinking,

00:17:09.790 --> 00:17:11.230
what they are supposed to do.

00:17:11.230 --> 00:17:14.155
So you need to provide them
something to go off of.

00:17:14.155 --> 00:17:15.780
BRIAN DORSEY: I have
a related question

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:17.079
around this exact thing.

00:17:17.079 --> 00:17:19.609
So if we think about
GUI versus command line,

00:17:19.609 --> 00:17:21.829
we kind of think that the
visual user interfaces

00:17:21.829 --> 00:17:22.829
are more discoverable.

00:17:22.829 --> 00:17:25.030
You can kind of figure out
what's possible or not,

00:17:25.030 --> 00:17:26.710
but you have to do the
same thing every time.

00:17:26.710 --> 00:17:29.335
And command lines, it's not easy
to figure out what's possible,

00:17:29.335 --> 00:17:31.170
but once you do, you
kind of internalize

00:17:31.170 --> 00:17:32.550
that and you can do
things very quickly,

00:17:32.550 --> 00:17:34.467
or you can make scripts
to do things in order.

00:17:34.467 --> 00:17:36.840
And I feel like we're
almost missing how

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:38.280
to think about these things.

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:40.800
And in particular for voice--

00:17:40.800 --> 00:17:43.230
as a user, I find this question
of not even knowing what's

00:17:43.230 --> 00:17:45.480
possible really challenging.

00:17:45.480 --> 00:17:47.310
I don't know what I
can say or not say

00:17:47.310 --> 00:17:49.090
to the thing I'm talking to.

00:17:49.090 --> 00:17:50.820
And it gets frustrating
after a while.

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:52.500
As people are interested
in designing these,

00:17:52.500 --> 00:17:53.670
do you have
recommendations for how

00:17:53.670 --> 00:17:54.640
to help with that challenge?

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:55.598
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yes.

00:17:55.598 --> 00:17:58.320
So the first and the biggest
recommendation that we offer

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:02.790
when we help design these
experiences is when the user

00:18:02.790 --> 00:18:06.370
ends up on your bot experience
or on your chat experience--

00:18:06.370 --> 00:18:09.540
however they are ending up
on that landing prompt--

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:12.150
that prompt should be
very specific in terms

00:18:12.150 --> 00:18:13.800
of what this bot can do.

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:19.680
So when my bot responds back
saying, hi, welcome to XYZ,

00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:24.240
I can help you get your balance
information and transfer funds

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:25.990
from one account to another--

00:18:25.990 --> 00:18:28.060
and if it does only
these two things,

00:18:28.060 --> 00:18:29.760
then mention those two things.

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:32.340
If there's any other
questions that they have,

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:34.530
at least the user now
knows that the bot

00:18:34.530 --> 00:18:35.940
can do these two things.

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:37.140
BRIAN DORSEY: Is this
like the rest of life?

00:18:37.140 --> 00:18:38.848
Like, setting expectations
is everything?

00:18:38.848 --> 00:18:40.280
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Exactly, right?

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:42.630
So just make sure that you're
setting those expectations

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:43.050
correctly.

00:18:43.050 --> 00:18:45.300
And obviously they're going
to ask questions that you

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:46.960
didn't expect them to ask.

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:49.860
And then for those, you
have the fallback mechanism

00:18:49.860 --> 00:18:52.110
where you say, OK, I
didn't understand that,

00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:55.860
but a connection to a
human being or a live agent

00:18:55.860 --> 00:18:57.580
is also very critical.

00:18:57.580 --> 00:18:59.880
There are going to be scenarios
where your bot will not

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:02.610
know what to do, because it's
not programmed to do that.

00:19:02.610 --> 00:19:05.920
In those cases, how you
connect to a live agent--

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:07.650
whether it's chat or a call--

00:19:07.650 --> 00:19:08.410
is important.

00:19:08.410 --> 00:19:10.620
And you need to figure
that out as well

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:11.880
while you're building the bot.

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:12.838
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:19:12.838 --> 00:19:15.150
I mean, for command lines,
we've got like Tak Tak Help.

00:19:15.150 --> 00:19:17.340
So usually you have
this verbose list

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:19.090
of all the things you can do.

00:19:19.090 --> 00:19:21.750
But in conversing with
AI, it doesn't exist.

00:19:21.750 --> 00:19:23.480
Is that something
that people have tried

00:19:23.480 --> 00:19:24.480
and it just didn't work?

00:19:24.480 --> 00:19:26.855
Is that something that just
doesn't resonate with people?

00:19:26.855 --> 00:19:29.250
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: I had
a conversation with one

00:19:29.250 --> 00:19:31.980
of our voice-user
interface designers

00:19:31.980 --> 00:19:33.630
at Google a few days ago.

00:19:33.630 --> 00:19:35.670
And she brought up a
very interesting point.

00:19:35.670 --> 00:19:38.340
She said, we all
assume that we know

00:19:38.340 --> 00:19:39.880
how to have a conversation.

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:44.610
And that's why we start
building a bot without creating

00:19:44.610 --> 00:19:46.890
any blueprints.

00:19:46.890 --> 00:19:48.900
And that is the biggest problem.

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:51.570
And she kept mentioning
and highlighting the fact

00:19:51.570 --> 00:19:54.780
that you need to build
the design flows.

00:19:54.780 --> 00:19:56.370
They're not flowcharts.

00:19:56.370 --> 00:19:57.990
They are the flows
where you say,

00:19:57.990 --> 00:20:02.520
here are the 1,000 ways in
which people can ask for coffee.

00:20:02.520 --> 00:20:05.040
And understandable
that machine learning

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:07.320
is going to handle
all those 1,000 ways,

00:20:07.320 --> 00:20:10.290
but you still need to
think how the flow will

00:20:10.290 --> 00:20:14.460
traverse when they are asking
for coffee in different ways.

00:20:14.460 --> 00:20:18.420
And conceptualizing and
visualizing that flow

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:19.510
is really critical.

00:20:19.510 --> 00:20:23.850
So she recommended that anybody
who starts designing a bot,

00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:27.900
do not start designing it
without having a blueprint

00:20:27.900 --> 00:20:30.360
of what you're designing for.

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:32.520
Here are the four things
I'm designing for,

00:20:32.520 --> 00:20:36.490
and then these four flows
can look something like this.

00:20:36.490 --> 00:20:38.220
And that is very
important to have.

00:20:38.220 --> 00:20:40.800
And I think that's the
part we keep missing,

00:20:40.800 --> 00:20:43.380
because we think that we know
how to have a conversation.

00:20:43.380 --> 00:20:45.660
And this is what people
are going to ask my bot,

00:20:45.660 --> 00:20:47.520
and we just end up developing.

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:49.020
BRIAN DORSEY: I'm
super new to this.

00:20:49.020 --> 00:20:50.820
When you say, design
these blueprints,

00:20:50.820 --> 00:20:52.890
I don't know what that
actually is or looks,

00:20:52.890 --> 00:20:56.780
because I'm used to know
either code or scripts or HTML

00:20:56.780 --> 00:20:57.457
or something.

00:20:57.457 --> 00:20:59.040
What's the format
of these blueprints?

00:20:59.040 --> 00:21:00.840
Is it just drawing
things out so you

00:21:00.840 --> 00:21:02.132
have a good sense in your head?

00:21:02.132 --> 00:21:03.350
Or what does it look like?

00:21:03.350 --> 00:21:05.225
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: I
asked the same question,

00:21:05.225 --> 00:21:07.500
because we are a little more
developer-centric people.

00:21:07.500 --> 00:21:09.083
So we think about
codes and scripts

00:21:09.083 --> 00:21:10.500
and flowcharts--
things like that.

00:21:10.500 --> 00:21:13.770
And she literally said
it can be any format

00:21:13.770 --> 00:21:15.150
that you want it to be in.

00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:18.340
It can be just a
simple script where

00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:20.730
it's like the user's
going to say this

00:21:20.730 --> 00:21:23.310
and the bot is going
to respond with this.

00:21:23.310 --> 00:21:25.930
And the bot can respond
in these four ways

00:21:25.930 --> 00:21:29.170
if the user says this question
in these different ways.

00:21:29.170 --> 00:21:33.450
So it can literally be
any format you prefer.

00:21:33.450 --> 00:21:35.160
It can be an Excel sheet.

00:21:35.160 --> 00:21:36.990
It can be just a Word doc.

00:21:36.990 --> 00:21:39.970
It could be in animated format
with pictures and stuff.

00:21:39.970 --> 00:21:43.110
But the idea is that you
should have something

00:21:43.110 --> 00:21:45.110
to go off of and not
just start coding.

00:21:45.110 --> 00:21:45.960
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
This is going to make

00:21:45.960 --> 00:21:48.980
it super awkward the next time
I run into Brian in the hallway.

00:21:48.980 --> 00:21:51.063
And I'm going to be like,
hold on, let me bust out

00:21:51.063 --> 00:21:54.080
my spreadsheet because I need
to map out this conversation.

00:21:54.080 --> 00:21:56.195
BRIAN DORSEY: You
don't do that already?

00:21:56.195 --> 00:21:58.380
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Well, yeah, do
you put that mental preparation

00:21:58.380 --> 00:21:58.880
in?

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:01.380
Because I promise you I am
now self-conscious about being

00:22:01.380 --> 00:22:02.735
able to have a conversation.

00:22:02.735 --> 00:22:04.110
Priyanka's like,
most of us don't

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:05.050
know how to have conversations.

00:22:05.050 --> 00:22:06.180
I'm like, yeah, of course.

00:22:06.180 --> 00:22:07.560
Why else would I host a podcast?

00:22:07.560 --> 00:22:10.730
I have no idea how to
have a conversation.

00:22:10.730 --> 00:22:12.420
But what you're
saying is we need

00:22:12.420 --> 00:22:17.190
some sort of flow of
dialogues, perhaps

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:19.930
in the form of a Dialogflow.

00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:21.340
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Exactly.

00:22:21.340 --> 00:22:22.930
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Did you
like that lead-in, by the way?

00:22:22.930 --> 00:22:23.710
I thought it was cool.

00:22:23.710 --> 00:22:24.210
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:24.210 --> 00:22:26.160
Been working on it
for like three days.

00:22:26.160 --> 00:22:29.700
So tell us a little bit more
about what Dialogflow actually

00:22:29.700 --> 00:22:30.300
is.

00:22:30.300 --> 00:22:33.450
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Dialogflow is Google's tool

00:22:33.450 --> 00:22:35.430
that is powered by
natural language

00:22:35.430 --> 00:22:37.680
understanding and natural
language processing

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:41.430
to create rich and
natural conversations.

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:45.420
And what I mean by that
is it makes this process

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:46.850
simpler and easier.

00:22:46.850 --> 00:22:49.920
When I said I can ask for
coffee in 1,000 different ways

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:53.250
depending on the day and
the mood and the time

00:22:53.250 --> 00:22:56.760
and all of that, it's
nearly impossible

00:22:56.760 --> 00:23:00.660
to gather all of those ways
in which 1,000 people are

00:23:00.660 --> 00:23:02.290
going to ask for coffee.

00:23:02.290 --> 00:23:06.210
But in a broader term, it's
easy to just lay it out.

00:23:06.210 --> 00:23:09.630
And what Dialogflow does
is you put an intent in,

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:12.600
saying most common ways in
which people ask for coffee.

00:23:12.600 --> 00:23:15.470
And then it would apply the
natural language understanding

00:23:15.470 --> 00:23:17.940
and NLP on it and
comprehend some

00:23:17.940 --> 00:23:20.070
of the other ways in
which people can actually

00:23:20.070 --> 00:23:21.130
ask for coffee.

00:23:21.130 --> 00:23:24.390
So I can say, I would
like some coffee.

00:23:24.390 --> 00:23:26.980
And I can give 10
examples in there and say,

00:23:26.980 --> 00:23:28.350
I would like some coffee.

00:23:28.350 --> 00:23:29.640
Can I get some coffee?

00:23:29.640 --> 00:23:31.890
It would be great to get
some coffee right now.

00:23:31.890 --> 00:23:34.650
Amazing to get some
coffee right now.

00:23:34.650 --> 00:23:36.330
Coffee sounds amazing.

00:23:36.330 --> 00:23:39.240
These are some ways in which
I ask for coffee depending

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:42.150
on what the day is
and what my mood is.

00:23:42.150 --> 00:23:44.880
I'm pretty sure Mark has
some very interesting ways

00:23:44.880 --> 00:23:46.218
in which he can ask for coffee.

00:23:46.218 --> 00:23:47.760
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
I mean, you're very

00:23:47.760 --> 00:23:48.780
polite to your coffee-maker.

00:23:48.780 --> 00:23:49.420
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:49.420 --> 00:23:51.400
Now I feel bad.

00:23:51.400 --> 00:23:53.160
I usually just yell
"coffee" at mine.

00:23:53.160 --> 00:23:55.020
And it's not
voice-enabled, I just

00:23:55.020 --> 00:23:57.775
am yelling coffee
while also wanting it.

00:23:57.775 --> 00:23:59.867
BRIAN DORSEY: And with
deep apologies to everyone

00:23:59.867 --> 00:24:01.950
listening to this before
they've had their coffee.

00:24:01.950 --> 00:24:02.908
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:24:02.908 --> 00:24:05.380
No, I mean, if you haven't
had your coffee, please pause.

00:24:05.380 --> 00:24:07.898
Go grab some-- ideally while
yelling the word, coffee.

00:24:07.898 --> 00:24:08.940
And then come back to us.

00:24:08.940 --> 00:24:09.780
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:09.780 --> 00:24:10.870
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Yes.

00:24:10.870 --> 00:24:14.370
So going back to it,
the power of Dialogflow

00:24:14.370 --> 00:24:17.370
is you provide these
intents, and then it

00:24:17.370 --> 00:24:20.310
would map some of the
others on its own.

00:24:20.310 --> 00:24:22.770
Because it's powered with
natural language processing

00:24:22.770 --> 00:24:25.230
and understanding,
it would understand

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:28.300
the other ways in which
people are asking for coffee.

00:24:28.300 --> 00:24:32.160
So you don't have to provide
those 1,000 examples.

00:24:32.160 --> 00:24:34.380
You can just provide
a few, and it still

00:24:34.380 --> 00:24:36.570
understands the user's intent.

00:24:36.570 --> 00:24:37.410
So that's one.

00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:39.720
And then the other
power of Dialogflow

00:24:39.720 --> 00:24:43.140
is you design your
bot experience once,

00:24:43.140 --> 00:24:46.680
and you can enable it for
multiple different interfaces.

00:24:46.680 --> 00:24:49.800
And that is the biggest
thing, because omnichannel

00:24:49.800 --> 00:24:52.770
is a huge requirement
for enterprises,

00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:54.990
because you want to make
sure that the experience

00:24:54.990 --> 00:24:57.840
of the brand is similar
on every channel

00:24:57.840 --> 00:24:59.610
that the user's
interacting you with.

00:24:59.610 --> 00:25:03.480
So whether they're coming from
Facebook Messenger or Slack

00:25:03.480 --> 00:25:07.260
or Google Home or Assistant
or just a web chat,

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:10.710
the experience should
be seamless and similar

00:25:10.710 --> 00:25:11.680
across the board.

00:25:11.680 --> 00:25:15.380
So that's the other power
you get from Dialogflow.

00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:17.740
There's also languages.

00:25:17.740 --> 00:25:19.670
So it's not that
you're only going

00:25:19.670 --> 00:25:22.670
to support English or one
language in which you're

00:25:22.670 --> 00:25:24.240
doing business today.

00:25:24.240 --> 00:25:26.330
You have to think
about global expansion

00:25:26.330 --> 00:25:28.980
and have multiple
languages supported.

00:25:28.980 --> 00:25:31.610
So if you're doing
it by yourself,

00:25:31.610 --> 00:25:33.620
it's going to be
really hard to support

00:25:33.620 --> 00:25:35.270
all these different languages.

00:25:35.270 --> 00:25:38.000
Dialogflow comes already
with 20 languages,

00:25:38.000 --> 00:25:40.670
and we keep adding the
support based on our text

00:25:40.670 --> 00:25:43.370
to speech and speech
to text offerings.

00:25:43.370 --> 00:25:45.325
So that is, again,
very powerful.

00:25:45.325 --> 00:25:46.700
And then it comes
with Analytics.

00:25:46.700 --> 00:25:49.050
And that's always very useful.

00:25:49.050 --> 00:25:53.640
The other very important aspect
is smalltalk and pre-built

00:25:53.640 --> 00:25:54.250
agents.

00:25:54.250 --> 00:25:57.290
So smalltalk is, as
you can imagine, like,

00:25:57.290 --> 00:25:58.490
what's the weather like?

00:25:58.490 --> 00:26:00.830
It comes with
pre-built smalltalk

00:26:00.830 --> 00:26:05.570
so that you can just plug the
smalltalk portions and intents

00:26:05.570 --> 00:26:08.120
into your bot
experience, so you don't

00:26:08.120 --> 00:26:11.110
have to think about all the ways
in which people do small talks.

00:26:11.110 --> 00:26:12.545
BRIAN DORSEY: Like
really chatty chat bots.

00:26:12.545 --> 00:26:14.085
PRIYANKA VERGADIA:
Exactly-- chatty chat bots.

00:26:14.085 --> 00:26:15.752
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Do
people respond well

00:26:15.752 --> 00:26:18.320
to the small talky chat bots?

00:26:18.320 --> 00:26:19.570
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: It depends.

00:26:19.570 --> 00:26:22.760
It depends on what the
persona of your bot is.

00:26:22.760 --> 00:26:25.700
And that is a huge topic
around persona of the bot.

00:26:25.700 --> 00:26:28.215
And you kind of
define that before you

00:26:28.215 --> 00:26:30.590
start building your bot--
whether your bot is going to be

00:26:30.590 --> 00:26:33.470
geeky or chatty or serious.

00:26:33.470 --> 00:26:35.930
And once you define
that persona,

00:26:35.930 --> 00:26:38.070
it might sometimes
make sense to have

00:26:38.070 --> 00:26:40.940
small talk experience in there.

00:26:40.940 --> 00:26:44.090
It usually makes it a little bit
more chatty but more friendly.

00:26:44.090 --> 00:26:46.670
BRIAN DORSEY: Can we make a
bot that only does small talk?

00:26:46.670 --> 00:26:48.350
SPEAKER 1: Welcome home.

00:26:48.350 --> 00:26:50.390
It's been awhile, hasn't it?

00:26:50.390 --> 00:26:52.886
You've certainly changed.

00:26:52.886 --> 00:26:56.150
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: I don't know.

