Yeah.

00:31:27.060 --> 00:31:29.370
This article is written
by Kaitlin Ardiff,

00:31:29.370 --> 00:31:31.890
who's one of our
strategic cloud engineers.

00:31:31.890 --> 00:31:34.620
And it's really basically
saying Cloud Scheduler

00:31:34.620 --> 00:31:36.210
handles the
scheduling part of it,

00:31:36.210 --> 00:31:38.670
so a task that happens
on a regular basis.

00:31:38.670 --> 00:31:41.050
And functions is the thing
we want to run in this case.

00:31:41.050 --> 00:31:42.930
And using Pub/Sub, our
messaging queue that's

00:31:42.930 --> 00:31:44.550
both high throughput
and very durable,

00:31:44.550 --> 00:31:46.883
including retries and things
like that so it doesn't get

00:31:46.883 --> 00:31:49.270
lost if the function has an
error or something like that.

00:31:49.270 --> 00:31:50.970
And so you use Pub/Sub
as the kind of connection

00:31:50.970 --> 00:31:53.040
between the two, which is
a really powerful model

00:31:53.040 --> 00:31:55.010
for distributed
systems in general.

00:31:55.010 --> 00:31:57.510
You've got a thing that is in
charge of policy, a thing that

00:31:57.510 --> 00:31:59.925
is in charge of making sure
that it happens at least

00:31:59.925 --> 00:32:01.425
once, and then a
thing that does it,

00:32:01.425 --> 00:32:03.008
which seems like a
really great setup.

00:32:03.008 --> 00:32:05.737
So it's a very nice,
succinct walkthrough

00:32:05.737 --> 00:32:07.320
of this pattern that
is really common.

00:32:07.320 --> 00:32:07.740
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Exactly.

00:32:07.740 --> 00:32:08.240
Right.

00:32:08.240 --> 00:32:10.350
It gives this example
using specifically

00:32:10.350 --> 00:32:12.420
a Python script
that runs BigQuery.

00:32:12.420 --> 00:32:15.390
But at the same time, you've
got your Manage Cron service.

00:32:15.390 --> 00:32:18.343
You've got a flexible
events handling system.

00:32:18.343 --> 00:32:20.010
And then you can
pretty much do whatever

00:32:20.010 --> 00:32:23.050
you want to do on the
actual execution side.

00:32:23.050 --> 00:32:25.410
So this is a really
starter architecture

00:32:25.410 --> 00:32:27.760
that can go out in a
lot of different ways.

00:32:27.760 --> 00:32:29.580
In this case, they're
using Pub/Sub,

00:32:29.580 --> 00:32:32.607
but you could also have
used HTTP for the functions,

00:32:32.607 --> 00:32:35.190
but I think the authentication
would be a lot harder for that,

00:32:35.190 --> 00:32:35.460
right?

00:32:35.460 --> 00:32:36.840
BRIAN DORSEY: It
is now supported

00:32:36.840 --> 00:32:40.210
in both scheduler and functions
to use the basic service

00:32:40.210 --> 00:32:40.710
account.

00:32:40.710 --> 00:32:42.540
And then you get the token
from the environment and kind

00:32:42.540 --> 00:32:43.740
of pass that through the HTTP.

00:32:43.740 --> 00:32:45.360
But I think that
going through Pub/Sub

00:32:45.360 --> 00:32:47.415
is a much more general pattern.

00:32:47.415 --> 00:32:49.290
And I think it lets you
plug other things in.

00:32:49.290 --> 00:32:51.767
Like in this case,
maybe you're not

00:32:51.767 --> 00:32:53.850
going to necessarily do
things off the same policy

00:32:53.850 --> 00:32:55.920
of every other hour or
twice a day or something.

00:32:55.920 --> 00:32:56.550
Maybe you are.

00:32:56.550 --> 00:32:58.560
And then you can add
additional things listening

00:32:58.560 --> 00:33:00.083
to that same Pub/Sub event.

00:33:00.083 --> 00:33:02.250
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah,
very flexible and a good way

00:33:02.250 --> 00:33:03.952
to start working on something.

00:33:03.952 --> 00:33:06.160
And the first time you build
it, you might be saying,

00:33:06.160 --> 00:33:08.077
well, I don't know if I
need all these pieces.

00:33:08.077 --> 00:33:10.080
Why not just keep it
very, very simple?

00:33:10.080 --> 00:33:11.850
But as you extend
outwards, I think

00:33:11.850 --> 00:33:14.452
you're going to find that having
built it in this flexible way

00:33:14.452 --> 00:33:16.410
is going to make it a
lot easier down the road.

00:33:16.410 --> 00:33:17.670
BRIAN DORSEY: I kind of
alluded to it at the beginning.

00:33:17.670 --> 00:33:19.620
There's some non-obvious
stuff that's great.

00:33:19.620 --> 00:33:22.830
If you have a direct
call and for some reason

00:33:22.830 --> 00:33:25.590
there's a time-out or something
goes wrong that's a temporary

00:33:25.590 --> 00:33:26.105
error--

00:33:26.105 --> 00:33:27.480
if you're making
the direct call,

00:33:27.480 --> 00:33:28.740
it will never happen again.

00:33:28.740 --> 00:33:30.587
If you're working off
of a queuing system

00:33:30.587 --> 00:33:32.670
and actually kind of
responding to those directly,

00:33:32.670 --> 00:33:35.550
it'll make sure that it retries
it after failure and the like.

00:33:35.550 --> 00:33:36.717
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Very cool.

00:33:36.717 --> 00:33:38.580
So all those details
are in the blog post.

